{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"disjoint_set","title":"Disjoint Set","group":"Advanced Structures","order":21,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A disjoint-set (union–find) structure keeps track of a collection of elements partitioned into <strong>non-overlapping groups</strong>, and answers two questions almost instantly: <em>which group is x in?</em> and <em>merge the groups of x and y</em>. It is the engine behind Kruskal's MST, cycle detection and dynamic connectivity.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Disjoint set (union–find)</strong> — a data structure over elements <code>0 … n-1</code> that maintains a partition into disjoint sets, each identified by one <strong>representative</strong> element, supporting <code>find(x)</code> → representative of <code>x</code>'s set, and <code>union(x, y)</code> → merge the two sets containing <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>The problem: dynamic connectivity</h3>\n<p><code>n</code> people are initially strangers. Two operations arrive in any order: <code>makeFriends(x, y)</code> — now <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> are in the same friend circle (friendship is transitive) — and <code>areFriends(x, y)</code> — are they in the same circle? Answer the queries as they come, with no knowledge of future operations.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 4; makeFriends(0, 1), makeFriends(1, 3), areFriends(0, 2), areFriends(0, 1), areFriends(0, 3)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>No Yes Yes</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th><code>makeFriends</code></th><th><code>areFriends</code></th><th>Problem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adjacency list + DFS/BFS</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n + m) — traverse the graph</td><td>a query must explore the whole component</td></tr><tr><td>Adjacency matrix + DFS/BFS</td><td>O(1)</td><td>O(n²)</td><td>same, and n² memory</td></tr><tr><td>Store the component id of every node</td><td>Θ(n) — relabel one whole component</td><td>Θ(1)</td><td>every merge rewrites up to n labels</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disjoint set</strong></td><td><strong>≈ O(1)</strong> amortised</td><td><strong>≈ O(1)</strong> amortised</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Represent every set as a <strong>tree</strong>: each element stores its parent, the root is the set's representative. <code>find(x)</code> climbs to the root. <code>union(x, y)</code> makes one root the parent of the other. Two elements are in the same set exactly when they climb to the same root.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Each set is a tree stored in a parent array; a root is its own parent</figcaption><pre><code>initially                 makeFriends(0, 1)        makeFriends(1, 3)\nparent: 0 1 2 3           parent: 0 0 2 3          parent: 0 0 2 0\n\n(0) (1) (2) (3)            (0)   (2) (3)              (0)    (2)\n                            │                         / \\\n                           (1)                      (1) (3)\n\nareFriends(0, 2): find(0)=0, find(2)=2 → No\nareFriends(0, 3): find(0)=0, find(3)=0 → Yes</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Basic implementation: parent array</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>make_set</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>parent[i] = i</code> for every element — <code>n</code> singleton sets.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>find(x)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Follow <code>parent</code> links until reaching an element that is its own parent; that root is the representative.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>union(x, y)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>rx = find(x)</code>, <code>ry = find(y)</code>; if they differ, <code>parent[ry] = rx</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>areFriends(x, y)</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>find(x) == find(y)</code>.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class DisjointSet:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        self.parent = list(range(n))       # every element starts as its own set\n\n    def find(self, x):\n        while self.parent[x] != x:         # climb to the root\n            x = self.parent[x]\n        return x\n\n    def union(self, x, y):\n        rx, ry = self.find(x), self.find(y)\n        if rx != ry:\n            self.parent[ry] = rx           # hang one tree under the other root\n\n    def are_friends(self, x, y):\n        return self.find(x) == self.find(y)\n\nds = DisjointSet(4)\nds.union(0, 1)                             # makeFriends\nds.union(1, 3)\nfor x, y in [(0, 2), (0, 1), (0, 3)]:\n    print(f\"areFriends({x}, {y}):\", \"Yes\" if ds.are_friends(x, y) else \"No\")\nprint(\"parent:\", \" \".join(map(str, ds.parent)))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass DisjointSet {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; parent;\npublic:\n    DisjointSet(int n) : parent(n) {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) parent[i] = i;      // every element starts as its own set\n    }\n    int find(int x) {\n        while (parent[x] != x) x = parent[x];          // climb to the root\n        return x;\n    }\n    void unite(int x, int y) {                         // \"union\" is a C++ keyword\n        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);\n        if (rx != ry) parent[ry] = rx;                 // hang one tree under the other root\n    }\n    bool areFriends(int x, int y) { return find(x) == find(y); }\n    const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; parents() const { return parent; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    DisjointSet ds(4);\n    ds.unite(0, 1);                                    // makeFriends\n    ds.unite(1, 3);\n    int queries[3][2] = {{0, 2}, {0, 1}, {0, 3}};\n    for (auto&amp; q : queries)\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"areFriends(\" &lt;&lt; q[0] &lt;&lt; \", \" &lt;&lt; q[1] &lt;&lt; \"): \" &lt;&lt; (ds.areFriends(q[0], q[1]) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"parent:\";\n    for (int p : ds.parents()) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; p;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class DisjointSet {\n        int[] parent;\n        DisjointSet(int n) {\n            parent = new int[n];\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) parent[i] = i;   // every element starts as its own set\n        }\n        int find(int x) {\n            while (parent[x] != x) x = parent[x];       // climb to the root\n            return x;\n        }\n        void union(int x, int y) {\n            int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);\n            if (rx != ry) parent[ry] = rx;              // hang one tree under the other root\n        }\n        boolean areFriends(int x, int y) { return find(x) == find(y); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DisjointSet ds = new DisjointSet(4);\n        ds.union(0, 1);                                 // makeFriends\n        ds.union(1, 3);\n        int[][] queries = {{0, 2}, {0, 1}, {0, 3}};\n        for (int[] q : queries)\n            System.out.println(\"areFriends(\" + q[0] + \", \" + q[1] + \"): \" + (ds.areFriends(q[0], q[1]) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\"));\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"parent:\");\n        for (int p : ds.parent) sb.append(' ').append(p);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class DisjointSet {\n    constructor(n) { this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) =&gt; i); }   // every element is its own set\n    find(x) {\n        while (this.parent[x] !== x) x = this.parent[x];   // climb to the root\n        return x;\n    }\n    union(x, y) {\n        const rx = this.find(x), ry = this.find(y);\n        if (rx !== ry) this.parent[ry] = rx;               // hang one tree under the other root\n    }\n    areFriends(x, y) { return this.find(x) === this.find(y); }\n}\n\nconst ds = new DisjointSet(4);\nds.union(0, 1);                                            // makeFriends\nds.union(1, 3);\nfor (const [x, y] of [[0, 2], [0, 1], [0, 3]])\n    console.log(`areFriends(${x}, ${y}):`, ds.areFriends(x, y) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\");\nconsole.log(\"parent:\", ds.parent.join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>areFriends(0, 2): No\nareFriends(0, 1): Yes\nareFriends(0, 3): Yes\nparent: 0 0 2 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) worst case per operation — the tree can degenerate into a chain</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Naive union can build a tree that is a straight line: <code>union(1,0), union(2,1), union(3,2), …</code> makes <code>find(0)</code> walk <code>n</code> steps. The two optimisations below exist to keep every tree shallow.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Union by rank (or by size)</h3>\n<p>Keep an extra array <code>rank</code> that stores an upper bound on the <strong>height</strong> of each root's tree. When uniting two roots, make the root of the <strong>shorter</strong> tree a child of the taller one — the height does not grow. Only when both have equal rank does the merged tree get one level taller.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Attach the shallower tree under the deeper root — height grows only on a tie</figcaption><pre><code>rank[rx] &lt; rank[ry]          rank[rx] &gt; rank[ry]          rank[rx] == rank[ry]\nparent[rx] = ry              parent[ry] = rx              parent[ry] = rx; rank[rx] += 1\n\n    (ry)                         (rx)                         (rx)  rank+1\n   /  │  \\                      /  │  \\                      /  │  \\\n ...  ... (rx)                ...  ... (ry)                 ...  ... (ry)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>What is compared</th><th>Guarantee</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Union by rank</td><td>height bound of the two trees</td><td>height ≤ <code>log₂ n</code> → <code>find</code> is <code>O(log n)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Union by size</td><td>number of elements in the two trees (attach smaller under larger)</td><td>same <code>O(log n)</code> bound; the size array is useful in its own right (component sizes)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Why log n</div><p>With union by rank alone, a tree of height <code>h</code> must contain at least <code>2^h</code> elements (each rank increase doubles the minimum size), so no tree is deeper than <code>log₂ n</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-up\"></i>Path compression</h3>\n<p><code>find(x)</code> already visits every node on the path from <code>x</code> to the root. While it is there, <strong>re-point each of them directly at the root</strong> — the next <code>find</code> on any of them is a single step. The parent array is only ever changed in a way that keeps the same sets.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Path compression flattens the path that find() walks</figcaption><pre><code>before find(6)                      after find(6)\n\n     (0)                               (0)\n     / \\                             / | \\\n   (1) (2)                         (1)(2)(4)        6 and 4 now point straight at 0;\n         │                              │   \\       5 still points at 4 (it was not on the path)\n        (4)                            (3)  (6) …\n       /   \\\n     (5)   (6)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 7; union(0,1) union(2,3) union(4,5) union(1,3) union(5,6) union(3,5); then find(6)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>parent array before / after shows the compression; connected(2, 5) = Yes</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class DisjointSet:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        self.parent = list(range(n))\n        self.rank = [0] * n                    # upper bound on the height of each root's tree\n        self.components = n\n\n    def find(self, x):\n        if self.parent[x] != x:\n            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])   # path compression: point straight at the root\n        return self.parent[x]\n\n    def union(self, x, y):\n        rx, ry = self.find(x), self.find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False                       # already in the same set\n        if self.rank[rx] &lt; self.rank[ry]:      # union by rank: shallower tree goes under the deeper root\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        self.parent[ry] = rx\n        if self.rank[rx] == self.rank[ry]:\n            self.rank[rx] += 1                 # only a tie makes the tree taller\n        self.components -= 1\n        return True\n\n    def connected(self, x, y):\n        return self.find(x) == self.find(y)\n\nds = DisjointSet(7)\nfor x, y in [(0, 1), (2, 3), (4, 5), (1, 3), (5, 6), (3, 5)]:\n    ds.union(x, y)\nprint(\"parent after unions:\", \" \".join(map(str, ds.parent)))\nprint(\"find(6) =\", ds.find(6))\nprint(\"parent after find(6):\", \" \".join(map(str, ds.parent)))\nprint(\"connected(2, 5):\", \"Yes\" if ds.connected(2, 5) else \"No\")\nprint(\"connected(3, 6):\", \"Yes\" if ds.connected(3, 6) else \"No\")\nprint(\"components:\", ds.components)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass DisjointSet {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; parent, rnk;                      // rnk = upper bound on the height of each root's tree\npublic:\n    int components;\n    DisjointSet(int n) : parent(n), rnk(n, 0), components(n) {\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n    }\n    int find(int x) {\n        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);   // path compression: point straight at the root\n        return parent[x];\n    }\n    bool unite(int x, int y) {\n        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);\n        if (rx == ry) return false;               // already in the same set\n        if (rnk[rx] &lt; rnk[ry]) swap(rx, ry);      // union by rank: shallower tree goes under the deeper root\n        parent[ry] = rx;\n        if (rnk[rx] == rnk[ry]) rnk[rx]++;        // only a tie makes the tree taller\n        components--;\n        return true;\n    }\n    bool connected(int x, int y) { return find(x) == find(y); }\n    void printParents(const string&amp; label) {\n        cout &lt;&lt; label &lt;&lt; \":\";\n        for (int p : parent) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; p;\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    DisjointSet ds(7);\n    int edges[6][2] = {{0, 1}, {2, 3}, {4, 5}, {1, 3}, {5, 6}, {3, 5}};\n    for (auto&amp; e : edges) ds.unite(e[0], e[1]);\n    ds.printParents(\"parent after unions\");\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"find(6) = \" &lt;&lt; ds.find(6) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    ds.printParents(\"parent after find(6)\");\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"connected(2, 5): \" &lt;&lt; (ds.connected(2, 5) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"connected(3, 6): \" &lt;&lt; (ds.connected(3, 6) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"components: \" &lt;&lt; ds.components &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class DisjointSet {\n        int[] parent, rank;                           // rank = upper bound on the height of each root's tree\n        int components;\n        DisjointSet(int n) {\n            parent = new int[n]; rank = new int[n]; components = n;\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) parent[i] = i;\n        }\n        int find(int x) {\n            if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);   // path compression: point straight at the root\n            return parent[x];\n        }\n        boolean union(int x, int y) {\n            int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);\n            if (rx == ry) return false;               // already in the same set\n            if (rank[rx] &lt; rank[ry]) { int t = rx; rx = ry; ry = t; }   // union by rank: shallower under deeper\n            parent[ry] = rx;\n            if (rank[rx] == rank[ry]) rank[rx]++;     // only a tie makes the tree taller\n            components--;\n            return true;\n        }\n        boolean connected(int x, int y) { return find(x) == find(y); }\n        String parents() {\n            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n            for (int p : parent) sb.append(' ').append(p);\n            return sb.toString();\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        DisjointSet ds = new DisjointSet(7);\n        int[][] edges = {{0, 1}, {2, 3}, {4, 5}, {1, 3}, {5, 6}, {3, 5}};\n        for (int[] e : edges) ds.union(e[0], e[1]);\n        System.out.println(\"parent after unions:\" + ds.parents());\n        System.out.println(\"find(6) = \" + ds.find(6));\n        System.out.println(\"parent after find(6):\" + ds.parents());\n        System.out.println(\"connected(2, 5): \" + (ds.connected(2, 5) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\"));\n        System.out.println(\"connected(3, 6): \" + (ds.connected(3, 6) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\"));\n        System.out.println(\"components: \" + ds.components);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class DisjointSet {\n    constructor(n) {\n        this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) =&gt; i);\n        this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);             // upper bound on the height of each root's tree\n        this.components = n;\n    }\n    find(x) {\n        if (this.parent[x] !== x) this.parent[x] = this.find(this.parent[x]);   // path compression\n        return this.parent[x];\n    }\n    union(x, y) {\n        let rx = this.find(x), ry = this.find(y);\n        if (rx === ry) return false;                  // already in the same set\n        if (this.rank[rx] &lt; this.rank[ry]) [rx, ry] = [ry, rx];   // union by rank: shallower under deeper\n        this.parent[ry] = rx;\n        if (this.rank[rx] === this.rank[ry]) this.rank[rx]++;     // only a tie makes the tree taller\n        this.components--;\n        return true;\n    }\n    connected(x, y) { return this.find(x) === this.find(y); }\n}\n\nconst ds = new DisjointSet(7);\nfor (const [x, y] of [[0, 1], [2, 3], [4, 5], [1, 3], [5, 6], [3, 5]]) ds.union(x, y);\nconsole.log(\"parent after unions:\", ds.parent.join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"find(6) =\", ds.find(6));\nconsole.log(\"parent after find(6):\", ds.parent.join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"connected(2, 5):\", ds.connected(2, 5) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\");\nconsole.log(\"connected(3, 6):\", ds.connected(3, 6) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\");\nconsole.log(\"components:\", ds.components);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>parent after unions: 0 0 0 0 0 4 4\nfind(6) = 0\nparent after find(6): 0 0 0 0 0 4 0\nconnected(2, 5): Yes\nconnected(3, 6): Yes\ncomponents: 1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(α(n)) amortised per operation — effectively constant</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Optimisations used</th><th>Amortised cost per operation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>none</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>union by rank only</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td>path compression only</td><td>O(log n)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>both</strong></td><td><strong>O(α(n))</strong> — the inverse Ackermann function, <code>≤ 4</code> for every <code>n</code> that fits in the universe</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Rank ≠ height</div><p>After path compression the <code>rank</code> values are no longer exact heights — they are only upper bounds. That is fine: the proof of the <code>log n</code> bound only needs ranks to be upper bounds, and leaving them untouched keeps <code>find</code> simple.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>The recursive <code>find</code> compresses the path but recurses once per level. Python's recursion limit (1000) can be hit on adversarial inputs <em>before</em> the first compression; an iterative two-pass version (first find the root, then re-point every node on the path) avoids that.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Detect a cycle in an undirected graph</h3>\n<p>Process the edges one by one. For edge <code>(u, v)</code>: if <code>u</code> and <code>v</code> are already in the same set, this edge closes a <strong>cycle</strong>; otherwise unite them. This is also exactly the test Kruskal's algorithm uses to decide whether an edge may join the spanning tree.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>n = 4, edges = (0,1) (1,2) (2,3) (3,1)</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>cycle found at edge 3-1</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>An edge between two vertices that are already connected creates a second path between them — a cycle. Union–find answers \"already connected?\" in near-constant time, so the whole scan is <code>O(E · α(V))</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class DisjointSet:\n    def __init__(self, n):\n        self.parent = list(range(n))\n        self.rank = [0] * n\n\n    def find(self, x):\n        if self.parent[x] != x:\n            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])\n        return self.parent[x]\n\n    def union(self, x, y):\n        rx, ry = self.find(x), self.find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if self.rank[rx] &lt; self.rank[ry]:\n            rx, ry = ry, rx\n        self.parent[ry] = rx\n        if self.rank[rx] == self.rank[ry]:\n            self.rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n\ndef has_cycle(n, edges):\n    ds = DisjointSet(n)\n    for u, v in edges:\n        if ds.find(u) == ds.find(v):                 # already connected → this edge closes a cycle\n            print(f\"edge {u}-{v}: already connected → cycle\")\n            return True\n        ds.union(u, v)\n        print(f\"edge {u}-{v}: joined\")\n    return False\n\nedges = [(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 1)]\nprint(\"graph has a cycle:\", \"Yes\" if has_cycle(4, edges) else \"No\")</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct DisjointSet {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; parent, rnk;\n    DisjointSet(int n) : parent(n), rnk(n, 0) { for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) parent[i] = i; }\n    int find(int x) { return parent[x] == x ? x : parent[x] = find(parent[x]); }\n    bool unite(int x, int y) {\n        int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);\n        if (rx == ry) return false;\n        if (rnk[rx] &lt; rnk[ry]) swap(rx, ry);\n        parent[ry] = rx;\n        if (rnk[rx] == rnk[ry]) rnk[rx]++;\n        return true;\n    }\n};\n\nbool hasCycle(int n, const vector&lt;pair&lt;int,int&gt;&gt;&amp; edges) {\n    DisjointSet ds(n);\n    for (auto [u, v] : edges) {\n        if (ds.find(u) == ds.find(v)) {              // already connected → this edge closes a cycle\n            cout &lt;&lt; \"edge \" &lt;&lt; u &lt;&lt; \"-\" &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; \": already connected → cycle\\n\";\n            return true;\n        }\n        ds.unite(u, v);\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"edge \" &lt;&lt; u &lt;&lt; \"-\" &lt;&lt; v &lt;&lt; \": joined\\n\";\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;pair&lt;int,int&gt;&gt; edges = {{0, 1}, {1, 2}, {2, 3}, {3, 1}};\n    bool cyc = hasCycle(4, edges);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"graph has a cycle: \" &lt;&lt; (cyc ? \"Yes\" : \"No\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class DisjointSet {\n        int[] parent, rank;\n        DisjointSet(int n) { parent = new int[n]; rank = new int[n]; for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) parent[i] = i; }\n        int find(int x) { return parent[x] == x ? x : (parent[x] = find(parent[x])); }\n        boolean union(int x, int y) {\n            int rx = find(x), ry = find(y);\n            if (rx == ry) return false;\n            if (rank[rx] &lt; rank[ry]) { int t = rx; rx = ry; ry = t; }\n            parent[ry] = rx;\n            if (rank[rx] == rank[ry]) rank[rx]++;\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n\n    static boolean hasCycle(int n, int[][] edges) {\n        DisjointSet ds = new DisjointSet(n);\n        for (int[] e : edges) {\n            int u = e[0], v = e[1];\n            if (ds.find(u) == ds.find(v)) {              // already connected → this edge closes a cycle\n                System.out.println(\"edge \" + u + \"-\" + v + \": already connected → cycle\");\n                return true;\n            }\n            ds.union(u, v);\n            System.out.println(\"edge \" + u + \"-\" + v + \": joined\");\n        }\n        return false;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] edges = {{0, 1}, {1, 2}, {2, 3}, {3, 1}};\n        boolean cyc = hasCycle(4, edges);\n        System.out.println(\"graph has a cycle: \" + (cyc ? \"Yes\" : \"No\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class DisjointSet {\n    constructor(n) { this.parent = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) =&gt; i); this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0); }\n    find(x) { return this.parent[x] === x ? x : (this.parent[x] = this.find(this.parent[x])); }\n    union(x, y) {\n        let rx = this.find(x), ry = this.find(y);\n        if (rx === ry) return false;\n        if (this.rank[rx] &lt; this.rank[ry]) [rx, ry] = [ry, rx];\n        this.parent[ry] = rx;\n        if (this.rank[rx] === this.rank[ry]) this.rank[rx]++;\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n\nfunction hasCycle(n, edges) {\n    const ds = new DisjointSet(n);\n    for (const [u, v] of edges) {\n        if (ds.find(u) === ds.find(v)) {                 // already connected → this edge closes a cycle\n            console.log(`edge ${u}-${v}: already connected → cycle`);\n            return true;\n        }\n        ds.union(u, v);\n        console.log(`edge ${u}-${v}: joined`);\n    }\n    return false;\n}\n\nconst edges = [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 1]];\nconst cyc = hasCycle(4, edges);\nconsole.log(\"graph has a cycle:\", cyc ? \"Yes\" : \"No\");</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>edge 0-1: joined\nedge 1-2: joined\nedge 2-3: joined\nedge 3-1: already connected → cycle\ngraph has a cycle: Yes</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(E · α(V))</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(V)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>This test is for <strong>undirected</strong> graphs only. In a directed graph the edges <code>a → b</code> and <code>c → b</code> put <code>a</code>, <code>b</code>, <code>c</code> in one set without any cycle; directed cycle detection needs DFS with colours (see Graphs).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-apps\"></i>Where disjoint sets are used</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-topology-star\"></i>Kruskal's MST</h3><p>Sort edges by weight; add an edge iff its endpoints are in different sets.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles\"></i>Connected components</h3><p>Union every edge; the number of roots is the number of components — <code>components</code> above.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Cycle detection</h3><p>An edge inside one set closes a cycle (above).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Dynamic connectivity</h3><p>Answer \"are x and y connected?\" while edges keep arriving — the friends problem.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>Image / grid regions</h3><p>Flood-fill alternatives: unite neighbouring pixels of the same colour (number of islands).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>Percolation, LCA (Tarjan), equivalence classes</h3><p>Any problem that merges groups and never splits them.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Union–find supports <strong>merge only</strong> — there is no efficient <code>split</code>. If sets must be separated again, it is the wrong structure (use offline processing in reverse, or a link-cut tree).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the amortised complexity of union–find with both optimisations, and what does α(n) mean?</summary><div><p><code>O(α(n))</code> per operation amortised, where <code>α</code> is the <strong>inverse Ackermann function</strong> — it grows so slowly that <code>α(n) ≤ 4</code> for any <code>n</code> below <code>2^65536</code>. In practice every operation is constant time. Tarjan proved this bound is tight for the structure.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Union by rank vs union by size — any difference?</summary><div><p>Both guarantee <code>O(log n)</code> tree height and <code>O(α(n))</code> with path compression. Size is more useful when you also need component sizes (e.g. \"size of the largest group\"); rank is a smaller number to store. Never attach arbitrarily — that is what allows <code>O(n)</code> chains.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does path compression work and why doesn't it break the sets?</summary><div><p>During <code>find(x)</code>, every node on the path to the root gets <code>parent[node] = root</code>. Membership is defined by <em>which root you reach</em>, not by the exact path, so re-pointing nodes at the same root changes nothing about the sets while making future finds shorter.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you detect a cycle in an undirected graph with union–find?</summary><div><p>Process the edges; for <code>(u, v)</code> check <code>find(u) == find(v)</code>. Equal → the edge connects two already-connected vertices → cycle. Otherwise <code>union(u, v)</code>. Total <code>O(E · α(V))</code>. It also counts components: <code>V − (number of successful unions)</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does Kruskal's algorithm rely on union–find?</summary><div><p>Kruskal adds the cheapest edge that does not form a cycle. The cycle test on each of <code>E</code> sorted edges would cost <code>O(V)</code> with DFS; union–find makes it near constant, giving <code>O(E log E)</code> total dominated by the sort.</p></div></details></section>"}